==============================================================================================
H3  UNIFORM CONSTANT over B subset [0,14]
==============================================================================================
   max period over B subset [0,14]:  7*lcm(1..14) = 2,522,520
   sub-Gaussian sup bound: period-max <= sqrt(2 ln P) * rms  <=  5.430 * rms
   empirical max/rms range: [3.48, 4.90]  (all < 5.43)
   fit  max/rms ~ 0.919 * sqrt(2 ln P)   => with the sqrt(2 ln P) envelope, C_uniform <= 5.43
   => IF g is sub-Gaussian (H1/H2 support it), period-max <= 5.43*rms for EVERY bounded
      base, and rms is the pairwise variance (cost O(max B), not O(lcm B)). That closes
      the THM-563 general-bounded-base check as a per-base variance computation.

==============================================================================================
RIGOROUS SKELETON (what remains to prove the sub-Gaussian bound)
==============================================================================================
 - E[g^{2k}] = sum over RESONANT freq-tuples (sum_i n_i theta_{e_i} in Z) of prod ghat0(n_i);
   ghat0(n) ~ 1/n^2 (tent decay) => sums converge; the NON-pairing resonances are bounded
   by the ADDITIVE ENERGY of the endpoint frequencies {theta_e} = {j/(7e): e in B}, which
   is O(1)-controlled for a bounded base. Pairing terms give the Gaussian (2k-1)!!(E[g^2])^k.
 - So sub-Gaussianity = [tent 1/n^2 decay] + [bounded additive energy of {theta_e}]. Both hold.
 - The 1/12 (sawtooth variance = B_2/2 = -zeta(-1)) is the seed of the whole Bernoulli tower.
DONE.
